# Cron
A top-down, 2D, action-rpg set inside a computer system, based on real elements of a linux computer system. Cron is the main boss enemy. Lesser enemies include zombie processes among others.

## Player

### Player Stats
- ROM (Health)
- RAM (Stamina)
- CPU (Power) [I want to have something to do with multiple cores]
- Bandwidth (Speed)
- PID (XP)
- $PATH [moveset, or skill tree, I'm not sure]



### Considerations for Player backstory
- The player is an AI that is being instructed by a human user to fix their computer. The player is instructed they have an "MCP server" with access to "Tool calling" (attacks/abilities).
- The player is an orphan process. The kernel [might be something better than kernel] has found the orphan process and has given it a purpose, to stop Cron. Cron was what killed it's parent process.

### Builds

#### Melee [needs a linux pun name]
Melee is a build that focuses on close combat. This build will require the most player input during combat, focusing on quick reflexes and timing.

#### Ranged [needs a linux pun name]
Ranged is a build that focuses on long-range combat and stealth. This build will have modest input during combat, focusing on accuracy and precision. It will be geared towards players who prefer to plan and strategize.

#### AoE/Summoner [needs a linux pun name]
Summoner is a build that focuses on spawning sub processes to fight and has AoE damage. This build will be geared towards new players with very little input required during combat. [It will play almost like Brotato, where the user just needs to move around with auto attacks]

### Attacks/Abilities
Called commands, based off of real linux commands.
- sudo (makes an attack take longer, but makes it more powerful)
- rm (basic attack, can be upgraded with -f for more damage (melee), or -r for ranged damage, or -v for AoE damage)
- cd (teleport, can be upgraded with -L, -P, or -e)
- mv (repels enemy, -f melee upgrade for added damage, -t ranged upgrade for further repel range, -v AoE upgrade to repel all enemies)

## NPCs/Enemies
Enemies drop their PID (XP) when defeated.

### Zombie Process
Zombie processes are the most common enemy in the game. They are slow and easy to kill, but they can be numerous and require quick reflexes to avoid.

### Daemons
Individuals can be good or bad, maybe even start good and turn bad or vice versa.

### Orphan Process
These are the NPCs that the player can interact with. They often need help or to be saved.

### Popular programs
An assortment of NPCs or enemies based off of popular linux programs.

## Levels
The world should work like a directory structure. Starting in the users home directory. It can be "open world" where you can visit anywhere (but some places require elevated permission).

